    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d138">

                <pb facs="0334" n="210" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg210"/>

                <head>No. 138.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Messrs. Swift and Trescot to Mr.
                        Evarts</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Commission to China</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1880-11-29"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 29,
                        1880</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: By the last dispatches and telegrams from
                    the commission, you were informed that two treaties had been signed, and that
                    Messrs. Swift and Trescot would leave for Shanghai on the 20th, and that Mr.
                    Trescot would bring over the treaties by the first Pacific steamer that he could
                    reach from this point.</p>
                <p>We left Peking on the 20th and reached Shanghai on the 27th. The steamer
                    connecting with the Pacific line at Yokohama leaving on the 30th, Mr. Trescot
                    will go in her to take the City of Peking at that port on the 8th December.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>We have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>
                        <list>
                            <item>JOHN L. SWIFT.</item>
                            <item>WM. HENRY TRESCOT.</item>
                        </list>
                    </signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
